sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch07-14> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Cellular automata in which a continuous change in initial density of black cells produces a discrete switch in long-time behavior, with whichever color was initially more common taking over the whole system, are fairly rare in one dimension, where domain boundaries often get stuck, but become increasingly common in two and more dimensions.";
    dc:type "computational-observation";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Density-driven discrete transitions are rare in 1D CAs, common in higher dimensions";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 7, “Mechanisms in Programs and Nature”; section “Origins of Discreteness” (from p. 337), p. 339. Scope: cellular automata in one, two and more dimensions started from random initial conditions of varying density"
}
